 **Formulas:**
-The sources describe the use of "immediately-adjacent neighbors" in the Y and Z dimensions to compute the gradient [S1]. For a voxel at $(x,y,z)$, the gradient components are computed from the Y and Z neighbors using standard central differences. The gradient vector $\mathbf{g}$ is then $\left[ \frac{\partial S}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial S}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial S}{\partial z} \right]$. The normal is derived from the normalized gradient. The specific encoding of the normal vector into the `normal_field` is handled internally by the VolPack library [S1].
